TCLAP 2.0.0 - Unreleased
========================

TCLAP 2.0 is a modern C++20 redesign under active development. It is not yet
stable: expect API breakages. For stable integrations, use 1.4 (C++98) or
1.6 (C++20 with an API mostly source compatible with 1.4).

API and migration highlights
----------------------------

* Parsers and arguments use aggregate specs with named fields; argument names
  are mandatory. Registration uses cmd.add() for borrowed arguments or
  cmd.addOwned<ArgType>() for parser-owned arguments. Self-registering
  constructors are removed. Borrowed arguments and constraints must outlive
  their use by the parser.
* Value access uses value(), and other getters use names such as name(),
  description() and argList(). ValueArg no longer converts implicitly to T.
* parse() returns ParseOutcome for success, help, version or a reported parse
  error. Stop application work on non-success outcomes. parseOrExit() is the
  command-line main() convenience. Invalid specifications still throw.
* Synchronous onMatch callbacks replace the Visitor hierarchy. Each parser
  owns its dialect and ignore-rest state; reset clears parsed state for reuse.
* Argument registration validates conflicts transactionally and uses declared
  metadata rather than virtual equality. OneOf/EitherOf/AnyOf provide grouping;
  xorAdd and the old XOR customization API are removed. Group metadata and
  command-tree snapshots are available for custom consumers.
* parse_value<T>() returns Expected and uses from_chars for supported numeric
  types, Unicode decoding for Unicode strings and stream extraction as a
  fallback. Numeric parsing accepts leading but not trailing whitespace;
  AutoBaseInt opts into C-style numeric bases.
* Text and DocBook help support hidden arguments, aliases, positional ordering,
  translated built-in messages and Unicode-aware wrapping.
* CMake 3.13 or later is required and installed targets propagate C++20.
  Release staging includes generated documentation, tests and fuzz sources;
  GitHub Actions covers Linux, macOS and Windows.

Commands and subcommands
------------------------
- Register nested commands with inherited global/parent options and local args.
- Explicit dispatch invokes a command action after successful validation; parse
  remains action-free. Text and DocBook help follow the selected command path.
- All options follow the complete command path; misplaced options report a clear
  error. Applications without commands retain their existing behavior.

Unicode text and wide input
---------------------------

* UTF-8/UTF-16/UTF-32/wstring values and native Windows wchar_t argv support.
* Locale-independent Unicode.h conversion utilities reject malformed text.
* Unicode short flags, combined switches and UTF-8 constraint metadata.
* Help wrapping preserves UTF-8 scalar boundaries; raw string bytes remain intact.

Ordered value constraints
-------------------------

* Ready-made LessThanConstraint, LessThanOrEqualConstraint,
  GreaterThanConstraint and GreaterThanOrEqualConstraint own comparison bounds
  and generate usage/error text through the predicate core.
* Inclusive RangeConstraint remains available with unchanged boundary behavior.
* Ordered comparisons reject NaN values/bounds and avoid bound arithmetic.

Callable constraints
--------------------

* PredicateConstraint adapts owned functions and function objects; independent
  description and short ID strings supply error and usage text.
* MakeRuleConstraint shares rule state with computed descriptions and short IDs.
* C++20 MakeConstraint supports capturing lambdas, move-only callable
  state and const invocation. Null function/member pointers and empty
  std::function objects are rejected.

Argument aliases
----------------

* Arg::addAlias and labeled specs' aliases field add equivalent spellings.
* Unicode short aliases work in bundles; all aliases share argument state.
* Detailed text/DocBook help lists typed aliases; metadata snapshots own them.
